Third-party components:

evald is a single static binary; it links these notable open-source crates. Each
retains its own license (Apache-2.0 / MIT unless noted); see the crate's source
for full terms.

* Apache DataFusion, Apache Arrow, and Apache Parquet (Apache-2.0) — the cold-tier
  query engine and the columnar block format. Pure-Rust (no C++), which is what
  keeps the static musl build clean.
* opentelemetry-proto (Apache-2.0) — the OTLP wire types (the single source of
  truth for the protobuf / OTLP-JSON decode path; never hand-rolled).
* redb (MIT/Apache-2.0) — the embedded ACID key/value store backing the
  trace_id->block index, the compaction watermark, and the universal Score store.
* tokio, axum, tower-http, hyper (MIT) — the async runtime and the OTLP/HTTP +
  query API server.
* prost (Apache-2.0) — protobuf decode/encode for the OTLP/HTTP-protobuf body.
* serde / serde_json / serde_yaml (MIT/Apache-2.0) — the OTLP-JSON path, the JSON
  API, and the YAML eval config.
* clap (MIT/Apache-2.0) — the headless-first CLI.
* rust-embed (MIT) — embeds the frontend SPA into the binary so the UI ships
  air-gapped, no Node toolchain.
* regex, chrono, uuid, hex, crc32fast (MIT/Apache-2.0) — the regex evaluator,
  the UTC time-partition key, score ids, id rendering, and the per-WAL-record
  checksum.
* tracing / tracing-subscriber (MIT) — internal structured logs.

-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Edition notes:

* The optional Tier-2 semantic backend (HNSW / embedding similarity) is gated
  behind a cargo feature and is OFF by default; it is deferred and not vendored in
  this release. Keeping it off by default is what keeps the default build inside
  the air-gap envelope (no network, no C/C++ dependency).
